<section>
  <h2>Frequently confused compatibility questions</h2>
  <h3>Does “supports BMW” mean it can code any BMW?</h3>
  <p>No. A physical interface carries a diagnostic connection; the application determines which functions it exposes, and the particular vehicle determines which modules and functions are present. An adapter may be perfectly capable of reading standard OBD emissions codes while a coding application refuses to communicate with its Bluetooth chipset or the selected BMW control module. Use the software publisher's compatibility lookup at the exact chassis, model year and platform. A retailer's blanket “all BMW” claim cannot substitute for that verification, particularly on older vehicle generations and on software that expects Ethernet.</p>
  <h3>What if an app lists a model only under Android or iOS?</h3>
  <p>Treat that qualification as part of the model's compatibility statement. It may reflect Bluetooth implementation, pairing differences, application development decisions or unsupported device features. Do not use a separate generic hardware specification to override an app developer's platform restriction. The filterable OBDLink table reports the manufacturer's supported host operating systems, while the BimmerCode-specific list provides an independent application-evidence tier. A model shown in the first table is not automatically accepted by every app available on that operating system.</p>
  <h3>Why do the Bluetooth names and plus signs matter?</h3>
  <p>Bluetooth LE and Bluetooth Classic differ in their connection behavior. Model suffixes can also designate different generations or product families. Thus OBDLink CX is not LX, vLinker BM+ is not automatically BM, and MC+ is not automatically MC Wi-Fi. Follow the actual model identification printed on the device and select the same option inside the app, rather than assuming a retailer listing or a thumbnail photograph is accurate. Firmware variations can alter connection procedures, so maintain an evidence date when recommending any model.</p>
  <h3>Is a more expensive or broadly supported adapter necessarily better?</h3>
  <p>No such general conclusion follows from the evidence. A product with more vehicle protocols may be useful for a multi-brand workshop, while an app-specific Bluetooth device may be simpler for a supported BMW-only use case. Compare costs only after checking the target operation, app entitlement, interface stability requirements and owner platform. Price, availability, shipping and firmware maintenance may change and have not been used as scoring inputs to this database. There is no hidden “best adapter” score or sponsored ranking.</p>
  <h3>Does physical pairing justify an ECU programming or reset attempt?</h3>
  <p>Absolutely not. Pairing and a successful read-only identification are communication tests; programming and adaptation require their own approved procedure, supported vehicle and module, suitable electrical conditions, compatible software and a credible recovery approach. Stop before writing if those conditions are unknown. The adapter database intentionally does not contain click-by-click ECU write instructions or promise that vehicle safety systems can be serviced through the same path.</p>
  <h3>How should this reference be maintained?</h3>
  <p>Update only after new primary evidence is recorded: the manufacturer model document, application developer adapter page, platform version and affected vehicles. If an existing row becomes uncertain, label it as needing re-verification rather than silently migrating a claim from a newer or similarly named product. Preserve the original source comparison, diagrams and distinctions until a reviewer confirms that the proposed destination truly answers its unique user task. That approach avoids repeating the same pairwise fragmentation that the recovery program is intended to address.</p>
